unidoc_construction_0017,7,page-8-chunk-1,8,0.5927709341049194,0.0427221953868866,"Let be the set of all needs and be the set of all tasks. As both needs and tasks are requirements, the set of all requirements consequently is defined by . Further, we define a realizedBy predicate such that iff requirement is realized by requirement . Obviously, this predicate is transitive; e.g., holds in Fig. 5. With these defini- tions, we can define a predicate for the point of interaction with iff there exists a set with such that for each . Accordingly, in our building control system , , , hold. To algorithmically determine these points of interaction, we initialize with the empty set for . Then, for each need ( ), we follow all realizedBy relations and add to the set for each task that is traversed. As a result, each task will have been marked by a set , from which can be computed according to the above formula. Because our approach only employs needs and tasks at this level, it can be conceived as being domain and method independent. Especially, this implies that whenever a traceabil- ity relation from features (i.e., needs) to responsibilities (i.e., tasks) exists in a given specifi- cation method or language, the above algorithm can be applied. An example for such a language is the User Requirements Notation (URN [20]), which provides the necessary means for requirements traceability. With the knowledge of the points of interaction, we are only able to systematically de- termine possible feature interactions, because dependencies at the level of needs and tasks do not necessarily imply that there will be interactions in the final system. For example, the task at a given point of interaction might never be used for realizing more than one need simulta- neously. Therefore, from the points of interaction of Fig. 5, only a possible feature interaction between the needs and (at ) as well as a possible interaction between , , and (at , , and ) can be derived. 2.2 Detection at Strategy Level In order to refine this set of possible feature interactions, dependencies between tasks by means of their strategies can be examined. This dependency occurs because strategies are coupled by signal types or attributes (see Fig. 3). Two observations can be made when using this information. N1 N2 N3 T2 T3 T5 T8 T7 Figure 5. Dependency Graph for Requirements T9 realizedBypoint of interaction T4 N4 T1 T6 NN 1 … Nn,,{}= TT 1 … Tm,,{}= RN 1 … Nn T1 … Tm,, ,,,{} R1 … Rnm+,,{}== Ri Rj→ Ri Rj N1 T4→ ϕ ϕ Ti() NT i() Ni 1, … Nik,,,{} N⊆= NT i() 1> Nj Ti→ Nij, NT i()∈ ϕ T1() ϕ T3() ϕ T4() ϕ T5() NT i() i 1 … m,,= Nt N∈ t 1 … n,,= Nt NT i() Ti Ti NT i() ϕ Ti() N1 N2 T1 N1 N2 N3 T3 T4 T5",2612
unidoc_construction_0017,8,page-10-chunk-1,10,0.5729449391365051,0.042087726294994354,"To illustrate, Fig. 6 shows an excerpt of an instantiation of the product model for the above example. The calculation of the paths starts at the control object type . Because two in- stantiations of this control object type exist, two possible paths ( and ) can be identified. As is the shorter one, it can be deduced that . With the technique for determining such paths, we can now eliminate the interactions that cannot occur because of the aggregation hierarchy. A modeling guideline of the PROBAnD method requires that control object types that implement tasks that are connected by realizedBy relations should be instantiated as closely as possible, because this reduces the number of signals that need to be routed through other instances in the aggregation hierarchy. Therefore, only tasks that are directly and not transi- tively realized by the task at the point of interaction need to be considered. If we assume that is the set of all tasks that are directly realized by . The set of all control object types that are on the path between the object type that im- plement tasks and the one that implements can be attainted by . If , then there can be no interaction between tasks and , because there are no common object types along the paths and as such different instantiations of the object type that implements the conflicting task are used. If the intersections of all possible combinations of are computed, all interactions that cannot occur because of the instantiation hierarchy can be eliminated. For the above example of task , is , and thus and , which leads to . Hence, there is no interaction at point . Whereas in the case of the following sets are computed: and , which leads to , Figure 6. Calculation of Paths in an Instantiation of the Product Model RoomCtrl:ControlObjectType illumCtrl:Instantiation IllumCtrl:ControlObjectType glareCtrl:Instantiation GlareCtrl:ControlObjectType agg