hquake se- quences and provided us with invaluable data to analyze this earthquake sequence. We thank Andrew Michael for his assistance with statistics. Jim Milne and Denise Onyskiw were helpful in working with Colorado Oil and Gas Conservation Commission databases and understanding injec- tion activities and regulations in Colorado. Matthew Weingarten provided helpful input and assistance in analyzing the Colorado injection data. Goran Ekström kindly provided the results of his analysis of the orientation of T25A. Jake DeAngelo assisted with New Mexico water production data. Thanks to Luke Blair who assisted with Geographical Information Systems and other mapping data. Keith Knudsen, Jeanne Hardebeck, and Jim Dewey provided constructive reviews of an earlier version of this manuscript. References Berglund, H. T., A. F. Sheehan, M. H. Murray, M. Roy, A. R. Lowry, R. S. Nerem, and F. Blume (2012). Distributed deformation across the Rio Grande rift, Great Plains, and Colorado plateau, Geology 40, no. 1, 23 –26, doi: 10.1130/G32418.1. Block, L. V ., C. K. Wood, W. L. Yeck, and V . M. King (2014). The 24 Janu- ary 2013 ML 4.4 earthquake near Paradox, Colorado, and its relation to deep well injection, Seismol. Res. Lett. 85, no. 3, 609 –624, doi: 10.1785/0220130188. Brodsky, E. E., and S. G. Prejean (2005). New constraints on mechanisms of remotely triggered seismicity at Long V alley caldera, J. Geophys. Res. 110, no. B04302, doi: 10.1029/2004JB003211. Cox, R. T. (1991). Possible triggering of earthquakes by underground waste disposal in the El Dorado, Arkansas area, Bull. Seismol. Soc. Am. 62, no. 2, 113 –122. Davis, S. D., and C. Frohlich (1993). Did (or will) fluid injection cause earth- quakes: Criteria for a rational assessment, Seismol. Res. Lett. 64, no. 3, 207–224. Efron, B. (1979). Bootstrap methods: Another look at the jackknife, Ann. Stat. 7, 1–26. Ekström, G., and R. Busby (2008). Measurements of seismometer orienta- tion at USArray Transportable Array and b